Notebook[{
Cell["Hypermatrix", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"043a1096-609e-cd47-a558-247d2567564f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"Hypermatrix", "[", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"arr1", ",", " ", "arr2", ",", " ", "..."}], "}"}], "]"}], 
   "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "a919053d-758c-164a-9bf7-8840dc7f06e7"],
 " ",
 "represents the given arrays as a hypermatrix, holding them in canonical \
order."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"4252f7c3-36b9-fa44-ad54-64415083150b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"Hypermatrix", "[", 
    RowBox[{"arr1", ",", " ", "arr2", ",", " ", "..."}], "]"}], 
   "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "b6d90279-6c3c-c54b-8b7c-53f8e94390d6"],
 " ",
 "is equivalent."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"94d6a68d-9748-484a-9738-1c470c30a2b5"],

Cell["Details and Options", "NotesSection",ExpressionUUID->"64f48663-a03b-b644-b655-d572de393e9a"],

Cell["\<\
The arrays are ordered by array order, lowest first; then by dimensions, \
shorter first, compared term by term; then by symmetry, taking \"Symmetric\" \
before \"Cyclic\" before \"Rigid\".\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"c7f6d60d-3d67-4545-b004-1e9f133088f7"],

Cell["\<\
Two arrays alike in all three are put in the Wolfram Language's own canonical \
order, so that a hypermatrix is a function of the set of arrays it was given \
and of nothing else. Giving the same arrays in a different order produces the \
identical expression.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"147f66a2-5320-af4c-b95a-a506b9218a93"],

Cell["\<\
An irregular array is necessarily \"Rigid\", since only a regular array can \
carry a symmetry, so the symmetry criterion separates the regular arrays \
among themselves.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"a36c7c54-edc8-a94c-9126-6ab0e5a0d6ba"],

Cell["\<\
Arrays of any kind of entry sit together freely. Raw entries given in place \
of an array are promoted to an ArrayObject.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"f9379a1a-c19f-bb45-8930-233282d03bf8"],

Cell["\<\
Length gives the number of arrays, and Normal the entries of each of them in \
order.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"301f8c5f-6d97-b744-b2b6-7bb6c2d5f372"],

Cell["Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"d767c412-ae5e-f349-92be-a92d36a4552d"],

Cell["The arrays are sorted whatever order they are given in:", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"8beb95e8-abba-6846-acfe-42f6eb31aa5e"],

Cell["\<\
hm = Hypermatrix[{
   ArrayObject[Array[1 &, {2, 2, 2}], \"Symmetric\"],
   ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}],
   ArrayObject[{1, 2}],
   ArrayObject[{1, 2, 3}],
   ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {2, 1}}, \"Symmetric\"],
   ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {2, 1}}, \"Cyclic\"]}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"61307577-056b-5d4c-a2c6-f73d63f31ea8"],

